13→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n  1414→    }\n  1415→  ],\n  1416→  \"glossary\": null,\n  1417→  \"overview\": {\n  1418→    \"structure\": {\n  1419→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1420→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1421→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1422→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1423→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1424→    },\n  1425→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1426→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the grip of a merciless plague known as the Red Death, a disease that spreads rapidly through the land, marked by bleeding pores and fatal scarlet stains. As society crumbles under its weight, Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and defiant spirit, retreats with a thousand courtiers to his secluded, fortified abbey. Sealed off from the outside world, the prince attempts to escape the dread of death by surrounding himself and his guests with opulence, music, and endless revelry, creating a false sanctuary of pleasure and denial. The abbey itself becomes a symbol of isolation and hubris, its seven rooms each adorned in a different color, culminating in a final, ominous black chamber with scarlet panes and a massive ebony clock that chimes with chilling precision.\\n\\nMonths pass in this gilded captivity, until Prince Prospero hosts a grand masked ball, a spectacle of decadence meant to mock the suffering beyond the walls. The ball unfolds in a series of vividly lit rooms, each glowing with blood-red firelight from stained-glass windows, creating an atmosphere of eerie beauty. The clock’s hourly chimes, however, serve as a grim reminder of time’s relentless march, halting all revelry and plunging the guests into silence. As the night deepens, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in blood and wearing a corpse-like mask. Unchallenged at first, the figure moves through the rooms with eerie calm, drawing no attention until Prince Prospero, consumed by rage and fear, draws his dagger and pursues the intruder through the chambers in a desperate bid to confront the embodiment of his denial.\\n\\nThe climax arrives in the black chamber, where the prince collapses dead upon confronting the figure—only to realize, too late, that the intruder is not a guest, but the plague itself, personified. The revelation is immediate and fatal: the Red Death had infiltrated the abbey all along, hidden in the guise of a masked reveler. As the clock chimes its final hour, the lights extinguish one by one, plunging the abbey into darkness. Every guest, including the prince, succumbs to the disease, their lives ending in silence and terror. The story concludes with the complete annihilation of the isolated elite, underscoring the futility of attempting to escape fate through wealth, power, or denial. The abbey, once a fortress of pleasure, becomes a tomb for all who sought to outrun death.\\n\\nThe narrative centers on Prince Prospero’s tragic arc—from a ruler who believes he can defy mortality through indulgence to a man undone by his own arrogance. His relationship with his courtiers is one of patronage and illusion, built on shared escapism rather than genuine connection. The turning point occurs when the Red Death, disguised as a masked figure, infiltrates the ball, exposing the fragility of the prince’s illusion. The final moments, where the lights die and the plague claims everyone, serve as a chilling resolution to the central conflict: no amount of wealth or isolation can shield one from the inevitability of death.\",\n  1427→      \"themes\": [\n  1428→        \"identity\",\n  1429→        \"ambition\",\n  1430→        \"loss\"\n  1431→      ],\n  1432→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1433→    },\n  1434→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1435→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1436→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1437→      